<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chester</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Deva Victrix</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chester is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheshire">Cheshire</a>, north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, on the River Dee near the Welsh border. It began as the Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> fortress of Deva Victrix, founded in the 70s CE, and became a key base against the Welsh. After 1066 it was the seat of the powerful Norman earls of Chester. Its walls, the most complete in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and its galleried medieval shopping streets, the Rows, still survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chester.jpg" alt="Chester" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tagishsimon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chester_-_Shops_in_city_centre_-_2005-10-09.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gruffudd ap Llywelyn reunited Wales from Gwynedd but was killed in 1063 after Harold Godwinson’s campaign, and from about 1081 Norman lords from <b>Chester</b> occupied much of the kingdom until a Welsh revolt in 1094 and the return of Gruffudd ap Cynan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-gwynedd">Kingdom of Gwynedd · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…for new causes: in the presidential campaign of 1880, &quot;If the Johnnies Get into Power&quot; backed the Republicans James A. Garfield and <b>Chester</b> A. Arthur against the Democrats Winfield S. Hancock and William H. English, and in 1914 a British version appeared as &quot;When Tommy Comes Marching Home&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/when-johnny-comes-marching-home">When Johnny Comes Marching Home</a></li><li><blockquote>The English Arabist Robert of <b>Chester</b> completed his Latin translation of the Liber de compositione alchemiae, generally considered the first Latin translation of an Arabic work on alchemy.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-11">11 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A Shrove Tuesday race on the Roodee at <b>Chester</b> is the first recorded at what is now Britain's oldest racecourse still in use.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-9">9 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Parliamentarian forces under Sydnam Poyntz inflicted a significant defeat on the Royalists under the personal command of King Charles I, with heavy losses, preventing him from relieving the siege of <b>Chester</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-24">24 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After crushing northern and Mercian resistance, William the Conqueror gave the earldom of <b>Chester</b> to Gerbod the Fleming and then, in 1071, to Hugh d'Avranches.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-palatine-of-chester">County Palatine of Chester</a></li></ul></section>
